  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Monday)\nIssued at 210 PM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nA trough and associated cold front are in the process of crossing\nthe CWA this afternoon, ushering in drier surface air and \ngenerally confining additional chances of showers and \nthunderstorms to our far eastern counties (Mellette, Todd, and \nTripp) through this evening. A few CAMs also try to fire something\nfrom far northeastern Wyoming into far northwestern South Dakota,\nand while confidence is low in this scenario occurring, have \nbrushed light PoPs across this corridor late this afternoon just \nin case. The parent low pressure center our trough/front are \nconnected to is ejecting off the Rockies across Montana, and will \ndeepen as it tracks northeast into Canada over the next couple \ndays. This will keep it too far north/east to offer us additional \ndirect impacts from thunderstorms, but a secondary trough \nconnected to this low will brush across the region Tuesday \nnight/Wednesday morning. So far, any shower/thunderstorm activity \nwith this secondary piece of energy is slated to remain well north\nof our CWA, but we'll see if that trends any further south in \nfuture model updates. Breezy conditions are expected ahead of this\ntrough across the northwestern South Dakota plains Tuesday \nafternoon, with gusts up to 40 MPH. Broadly speaking, our focus \nfor much of the work week will be on continued warm and dry \nconditions, which will continue to support elevated to near- \ncritical fire weather conditions each day.\n\nWednesday onward, somewhat zonal flow through the column is \nexpected until we load up a fresh pair of low pressure systems \nFriday into the weekend...one crossing Canada to our north and a \nstronger system moving into the northern Rockies. The Canadian \nlow may offer us a few showers Friday into Friday night, before \nthe stronger second low brings our next organized chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms Saturday and Sunday. This system will\nmoisten up our relative humidity values taking the edge off fire\nweather concerns, and COULD spell at least a temporary end to the\nheat! NBM high temperatures currently reach 75 to 85 degrees \nSaturday, but then hold in the mid-60s to mid-70s Sunday, and the \nlow 60s to low 70s Monday. Unsettled conditions will continue \nthrough this time as the low will not yet have cleared the \nnorthern Plains. Keep in mind that our actual eventual \ntemperatures will be tied to the strength and track of this low, \nso stay tuned as we monitor for any trends. 500mb height cluster \nanalysis currently supports the 12z deterministic GFS/ECMWF pretty\nstrongly (our current forecast), but one cluster (11-13 percent \nof the ensemble space Sunday and Monday, Canadian-heavy) leans \ninto a weaker/further north track with more of a ridging signature\nover our region, which would keep temperatures considerably \nwarmer and reduce potential QPF amounts. Another cluster each day \n(another 25 percent of the ensemble space) leans somewhat warmer \n(and drier) than the other clusters as well. Something to watch.",
